["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Upland birds flush at your feet and are gone in a second. If you know the shape, the tail and the habitat before you go, you name the bird as it lifts.</p></aside><ul><li>Each screen shows one bird or one group, male and female where we found both.</li><li>Habitat is half the ID: aspen and birch for ruffed grouse, dark conifers for spruce grouse, open ridges for dusky grouse, rock and cheatgrass for chukar <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Some look alikes have no season at all. Those are on the last photo screen.</li><li>Photos are illustrations. The written field marks are the source of truth.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ruffed-grey.jpg\" alt=\"Grey phase male displaying, Peace River country, BC. Grey tail, black band.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grey phase male displaying, Peace River country, BC. Grey tail, black band. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: 72426950scott, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruffed_Grouse_(1).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/ruffed-grouse-hen.jpg\" alt=\"In fall leaves. Barred brown, small crest, fan tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>In fall leaves. Barred brown, small crest, fan tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mdf, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bonasa-umbellus-001edit1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ruffed-snow.jpg\" alt=\"In snow, Oregon. Crest, barred body, long tail with a dark band.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>In snow, Oregon. Crest, barred body, long tail with a dark band. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Oregon Department of Fish &amp; Wildlife, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1553_ruffed_grouse_matthews_odfw_(4423732628).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ruffed-tree.jpg\" alt=\"In a tree, Glacier National Park. Grouse eat buds up in aspen and birch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>In a tree, Glacier National Park. Grouse eat buds up in aspen and birch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: GlacierNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruffed_Grouse_-_Bonasa_umbellus_(23883619240).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Grey and red (brown) colour phases. The tail band is the same in both <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Wide dark band near the end of a long fan shaped tail <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Explodes off the ground with a roar of wings <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/dusky-grouse-male.jpg\" alt=\"Dusky male displaying in sagebrush. Slate grey, red air sacs, yellow orange comb.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Dusky male displaying in sagebrush. Slate grey, red air sacs, yellow orange comb. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: YellowstoneNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusky_grouse_male_in_the_sagebrush_(53482944105).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/dusky-grouse-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Dusky hen in aspen. Mottled brown and grey, long dark tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Dusky hen in aspen. Mottled brown and grey, long dark tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Great Sand Dunes National Park and Preserve, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusky_Grouse_in_Aspen_Tree_(51699770724).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-sooty-male.jpg\" alt=\"Sooty male displaying, Washington. The coast form.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sooty male displaying, Washington. The coast form. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Walter Siegmund (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dendragapus_fuliginosus_5058.JPG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-sooty-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Sooty hen, California. Mottled, long dark tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sooty hen, California. Mottled, long dark tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: LassenNPS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sooty_Grouse-_Dendragapus_fuliginosus_(9403816948).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The biggest forest grouse in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Sooty grouse is the coast form; the synopsis lists it in Region 2 as \"SOOTY (Blue)\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Male: plain slate grey with a pale grey band at the tail tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/2e/Dusky_Grouse.webm/Dusky_Grouse.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>A male dusky (blue) grouse showing off for a hen. <span class=\"credit\">Video: Nomdeploom, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dusky_Grouse.webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-spruce-male.jpg\" alt=\"Male displaying, Ontario. Black throat and breast, red comb.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Male displaying, Ontario. Black throat and breast, red comb. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mdf, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Falcipennis-canadensis-002.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/spruce-grouse-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen. Heavy black and white barring on brown.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen. Heavy black and white barring on brown. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan Covington, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canachites_canadensis_323749881.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Dark conifer forest, often on the ground near spruce and lodgepole pine <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Very tame; it often just sits and watches you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>In southern BC the male shows white spots above a dark tail: the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Franklin's grouse\">Franklin's</button> form <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/sharp-tailed-grouse.jpg\" alt=\"Sharp tailed grouse in summer grass. Pale, spotted, short pointed tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sharp tailed grouse in summer grass. Pale, spotted, short pointed tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Cephas, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tympanuchus_phasianellus_campestris_GNP_03.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/sharp-tailed-grouse-snow.jpg\" alt=\"Sharp tailed grouse in snow near Fort St. John, BC.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sharp tailed grouse in snow near Fort St. John, BC. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Gerry from Fort St. John, BC, Canada, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tympanuchus_phasianellus_in_snow.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ptarmigan-summer.jpg\" alt=\"White tailed ptarmigan in summer, Mount Rainier. Mottled, on alpine rock.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tailed ptarmigan in summer, Mount Rainier. Mottled, on alpine rock. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS - Pacific Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Rainier_White-Tailed_Ptarmigan_(53058740798).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-ptarmigan-winter.jpg\" alt=\"Willow ptarmigan in winter, Alaska. White, with black tail feathers.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Willow ptarmigan in winter, Alaska. White, with black tail feathers. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Colin Canterbury/USFWS, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willow_ptarmigan_winter_plumage.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Sharp tailed grouse: pale, with a short pointed tail that shows white in flight <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>White tailed ptarmigan: white tail in every season; willow and rock ptarmigan have black tails <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Both are legal only in listed MUs (Management Units) and places <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-quail-male-vernon.jpg\" alt=\"Male, Vernon, BC. Black face edged in white, forward curling plume.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Male, Vernon, BC. Black face edged in white, forward curling plume. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan D. Wilson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_(Male).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-quail-female.jpg\" alt=\"Female with a chick. Grey brown face, small plume.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Female with a chick. Grey brown face, small plume. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan Vernon, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Callipepla_californica_-California,_USA_-female_and_chick-_8.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-quail-pair.jpg\" alt=\"Male (right) and female, Nevada.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Male (right) and female, Nevada. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: BLM Nevada, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_Fallon_NV_(19978763835).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-quail-vernon.jpg\" alt=\"Quail country near Vernon: brushy draws and farm edges.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Quail country near Vernon: brushy draws and farm edges. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Alan D. Wilson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:California_Quail_(Male).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Both sexes carry the teardrop plume; the male's is bigger and black <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>They live in a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Covey\">covey</button> and run before they fly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Legal in Region 8, not in Region 3 (99%, Region 3 page 34 has no quail row).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/c/cf/Male_California_quail_%28Callipepla_californica%29.webm/Male_California_quail_%28Callipepla_californica%29.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>Male California quail: black face, curled plume. <span class=\"credit\">Video: devra, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_California_quail_(Callipepla_californica).webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-chukar.jpg\" alt=\"Chukar. Red bill and legs, black necklace, barred flanks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Chukar. Red bill and legs, black necklace, barred flanks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Artemy Voikhansky, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chukar_partridge.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-pheasant-rooster.jpg\" alt=\"Pheasant rooster, South Dakota. Green head, red face, white ring, long tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pheasant rooster, South Dakota. Green head, red face, white ring, long tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ring-necked_pheasant_at_LaCreek_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(51154749494).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-pheasant-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Pheasant hen, England. Buff brown, long pointed tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pheasant hen, England. Buff brown, long pointed tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_pheasant_(Phasianus_colchicus)_hen.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Chukar: steep rocky slopes and cheatgrass; runs uphill, flies downhill <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Pheasant: farm edges, ditches and cattails. Check your region's rules on hens (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> for your region).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-turkey-tom.jpg\" alt=\"Tom strutting, Zion National Park, Utah. Fanned tail, bare head, chest beard.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tom strutting, Zion National Park, Utah. Fanned tail, bare head, chest beard. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Zion National Park, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wild_Turkey_(6384983627).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-turkey-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen, Virginia. Smaller, browner, feathered blue grey head.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen, Virginia. Smaller, browner, feathered blue grey head. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Judy Gallagher, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wild_Turkey_(female)_-_Meleagris_gallopavo,_Occoquan_Bay_National_Wildlife_Refuge,_Woodbridge,_Virginia.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-turkey-flock.jpg\" alt=\"Flock, California. Check each bird for a beard.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Flock, California. Check each bird for a beard. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Missvain, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meleagris_gallopavo_-_November_2024_-_Sarah_Stierch_02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Spring seasons are for bearded birds only (98%, turkey session).</li><li>A bearded turkey has a tuft of hair like feathers on the chest (98%, page 3). A few hens grow beards <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/snowshoe-hare-summer.jpg\" alt=\"Summer coat, Banff. Grey brown, dark ear tips.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Summer coat, Banff. Grey brown, dark ear tips. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Martin Kraft, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MK04608_Snowshoe_Hare_(Banff).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/snowshoe-hare-winter.jpg\" alt=\"Winter coat. White, with huge furred hind feet.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Winter coat. White, with huge furred hind feet. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: D. Gordon E. Robertson, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snowshoe_Hare,_Shirleys_Bay.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Turns white in fall and brown in spring, by day length, not snow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>In a snowless fall a white hare stands out on brown ground (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-sage-grouse.jpg\" alt=\"Greater sage grouse male displaying. Spiky tail, white chest.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Greater sage grouse male displaying. Spiky tail, white chest. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Bureau of Land Management, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_sage-grouse_(Centrocercus_urophasianus).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</div></aside>","<ul><li>Sage grouse: no season row anywhere in the synopsis, so no open season in BC (90%, my reading). It is also listed as gone from BC (Tip).</li></ul><ul><li>Mountain quail: \"No open season on mountain quail\" (99%, Region 1, page 21). It has a straight plume, not a curled one <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Sharp tailed grouse outside the listed MUs: no season <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. See the Grouse session.</li></ul>","<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Name the bird by its tail and its trees. If it flushed from the wrong habitat, look twice.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Shooting a sharp tailed grouse because it \"looked like a grouse\" in an MU with no season.</li><li>Calling a dusky hen a ruffed grouse. Look for the black tail band.</li><li>Shooting a hen pheasant where only roosters are legal.</li></ul></aside>","<aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><ul><li>Every bird you flush, call out the tail: fan with a band, plain dark, pointed, or white.</li><li>Note the trees and ground where it rose. Match it to the habitat on this screen.</li><li>For turkeys in spring, find the beard through binoculars before anything else.</li></ul></aside>","<ul><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition</a>, read directly: bearded turkey definition page 3; mountain quail line Region 1 page 21; Region 3 page 34 (no quail row); upland season tables by region. Checked 2026-10-01 to 2026-10-02 (97 to 99%).</li><li>Field marks follow the Grouse, Quail chukar partridge pheasant, Wild turkey and Small game sessions in this app, and the <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/field-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audubon field guide</a> (75 to 80%).</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, public domain or Creative Commons. Credits under each photo.</li></ul>"]